<section class="wrap outcome" id="outcome" aria-labelledby="oc-h">
  <p class="eyebrow">What this approach gives</p>
  <h2 id="oc-h" style="margin-top:8px">One starting point instead of several</h2>
  <div class="oc-grid">
    <div>
      <h3>One place to start</h3>
      <p>Every question has one first stop, and the two disciplines stay side by side there.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Owners keep their content</h3>
      <p>The front door links to what owners maintain, so nothing drifts out of date in two places.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>A check you can repeat</h3>
      <p>The find-it task at the top is cheap to run with real menus before and after any merge.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="reflect">Consolidation isn't the goal in itself. <span>In my experience, a single front door works when it's organized around the questions people actually arrive with.</span></p>
</section>

<section class="wrap claims" id="claims" aria-labelledby="h-claims">
  <p class="eyebrow">What it can claim</p>
  <h2 id="h-claims" style="margin-top:8px">What this story can support, and what it can't yet</h2>
  <p>I graded these myself. Every claim is about this approach or this page, so you can check most of them from here.</p>
  <details class="claimsbox"><summary>Claims register · 5 claims, and how well each is supported</summary>
  <dl class="legend">
    <div><dt><span class="cl">observed</span></dt><dd>Visible on this page.</dd></div>
